  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "claim_9e721e5314894961a0dd86f97f0f6784",
      "statement": "Brijio's core trust assumptions are that the user explicitly starts/stops the bridge, tokens are held only by authorized parties, the browser session belongs to the user, and the relay transports messages without needing to inspect content; these are stated in the threat model and treated as non-negotiable invariants in AGENTS.md.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://AGENTS.md#L48-L72",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:2f1e3ef97f648cdd78b07a17e495ba36a58cd7b4943a57a65ef317a4cf572c51:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/security/THREAT_MODEL.md#L57-L63",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:88cfb64eb058b2de0e6e8c0b5c9a42091dcaf5c4cc68a25b1f6d4eac035a841b:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_4895002fe692420999579edd4439c851",
      "statement": "The threat model enumerates four threat classes — malicious agent, compromised relay server, network attacker, and malicious website — with architectural mitigations (explicit request/response protocol, no continuous streaming, user-controlled connection, progressive disclosure, TLS, separate tokens, least-privilege permissions, structured read-not-evaluated content) rather than purely defensive code patterns.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/security/THREAT_MODEL.md#L13-L56",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:5a7e88c406110a553c2394d39c39e919e42feb1835425473807bfc3d73120285:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_9c233fa5c4324a9e85dbeba224ae3187",
      "statement": "The trust-boundaries document maps four boundaries (Agent↔MCP server, MCP server↔relay, relay↔extension, extension↔browser session), places trust in pairing token, MCP auth token, relay routing, and user control, and explicitly does not assume trust for network, cloud-relay privacy, agent intent, or website content; its guiding rule asks whether a new feature expands trust in a party that should not be trusted.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/security/TRUST_BOUNDARIES.md#L7-L73",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:c535e58aed051e5a9b16ba16bc6fd63b6c799e289f9c3f4161fd377f6a375dbd:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_fb39ae4149c14da7b4fb6c2b67ebd73f",
      "statement": "The WebSocket relay requires BRIJIO_PAIRING_TOKEN (or BRIJIO_TOKEN) at startup, rejects unauthenticated messages with auth_required and wrong tokens with auth_failed, derives a scopeKey from the pairing token, and routes responses back to the waiting MCP socket by scope plus message id without inspecting payloads; the MCP server and extension use separate tokens so agent authorization and browser identity are independent trust facts.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/security/TRUST_BOUNDARIES.md#L16-L31",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:0d91c61aa279f28ac5e581451015ff4597171a4bf00636c07a4b0532cd981af8:"
